**Semantics to implement.**
- States: `PENDING`, `FULFILLED(value)`, `REJECTED(error)`. A promise settles at most once.
- `then(onFulfilled, onRejected)` returns a **new** promise. Its result is the handler's return value; if the handler throws, the new promise is rejected. If there is no handler for the outcome, the value or error passes through.
- Handlers registered before settlement are queued; those registered after run right away (on the executor, not inline, to mimic async delivery).

**Sketch:**

```java
class Promise<T> {
  enum State { PENDING, FULFILLED, REJECTED }
  private State state = State.PENDING;
  private T value; private Throwable error;
  private final List<Runnable> callbacks = new ArrayList<>();
  private final Executor ex;

  Promise(Executor ex, BiConsumer<Consumer<T>, Consumer<Throwable>> executor) {
    this.ex = ex;
    try { executor.accept(this::resolve, this::reject); }
    catch (Throwable t) { reject(t); }
  }
  synchronized void resolve(T v) { settle(State.FULFILLED, v, null); }
  synchronized void reject(Throwable e) { settle(State.REJECTED, null, e); }
  private void settle(State s, T v, Throwable e) {
    if (state != State.PENDING) return;      // settle once
    state = s; value = v; error = e;
    callbacks.forEach(c -> ex.execute(c)); callbacks.clear();
  }
  <R> Promise<R> then(Function<T,R> ok, Function<Throwable,R> fail) {
    return new Promise<>(ex, (res, rej) -> {
      Runnable r = () -> { try {
        if (state == State.FULFILLED) res.accept(ok != null ? ok.apply(value) : (R) value);
        else if (fail != null) res.accept(fail.apply(error)); else rej.accept(error);
      } catch (Throwable t) { rej.accept(t); } };
      synchronized (this) { if (state == State.PENDING) callbacks.add(r); else ex.execute(r); }
    });
  }
}
```

**Improvements to discuss:** `catch`/`finally`, `Promise.all` (a counter plus a result array), `Promise.race`, adopting a promise returned from a handler (flattening), unhandled-rejection reporting, and the fact that Java already provides `CompletableFuture`, so a real project would use it.
